The statement is **true**.

**Proof.**

Let $u \in H^1(\mathbb{R})$. Recall that every $H^1(\mathbb{R})$ function admits an (absolutely continuous) representative, still denoted $u$, satisfying
$$u(x+h)-u(x)=\int_0^h u'(x+t)\,dt,$$
where $u'\in L^2(\mathbb{R})$ is the weak derivative. Hence the difference quotient can be written as
$$\frac{u(x+h)-u(x)}{h}=\frac{1}{h}\int_0^h u'(x+t)\,dt,$$
and therefore
$$\frac{u(x+h)-u(x)}{h}-u'(x)=\frac{1}{h}\int_0^h \bigl(u'(x+t)-u'(x)\bigr)\,dt.$$

By Jensen's inequality (applied to the probability measure $\frac{1}{h}dt$ on $[0,h]$) and Fubini,
$$\left\|\frac{u(\cdot+h)-u(\cdot)}{h}-u'\right\|_{L^2}^2
\le \frac{1}{h}\int_0^h \bigl\|u'(\cdot+t)-u'(\cdot)\bigr\|_{L^2}^2\,dt.$$

Now we use the **continuity of translations in $L^2$**: for every $f\in L^2(\mathbb{R})$,
$$\|f(\cdot+t)-f(\cdot)\|_{L^2}\to 0 \quad\text{as }t\to 0.$$
(This is standard: it holds first for $f\in C_c(\mathbb{R})$ by uniform continuity, and then for all $f\in L^2$ by density of $C_c$ in $L^2$ and the uniform bound $\|f(\cdot+t)-f(\cdot)\|_2\le 2\|f\|_2$.)

Applying this to $f=u'\in L^2(\mathbb{R})$, define $g(t):=\|u'(\cdot+t)-u'(\cdot)\|_{L^2}^2$. Then $g(t)\to 0$ as $t\to 0$, and $g$ is bounded. Consequently,
$$0\le \frac{1}{h}\int_0^h g(t)\,dt \to 0 \quad\text{as }h\to 0,$$
since the Cesàro average of a function tending to $0$ at the origin also tends to $0$.

Thus
$$\left\|\frac{u(\cdot+h)-u(\cdot)}{h}-u'\right\|_{L^2}\to 0 \quad\text{as }h\to 0,$$
which is precisely the claimed $L^2$-convergence of the difference quotient to the weak derivative. $\blacksquare$

